Binary Search – Basics
🔹 What is Binary Search?

A searching algorithm used to find an element in a sorted array/list.

Works on the principle of divide and conquer.

Instead of checking every element, it repeatedly divides the search interval into halves.

🔹 Why Apply Binary Search?

Efficiency: Much faster than linear search.

Time Complexity:

Binary Search → O(log{base 2} n)

Linear Search → O(n)

Reduces number of comparisons drastically, especially for large datasets.

🔹 When to Apply Binary Search?

When the array/list is sorted (in ascending or descending order).

When random access to elements is possible (like arrays, not linked lists).

Real life example is Dictionary

Iterative code of Binary Search :
Pseudo Code: f(arr,n,target)
             {
                low=0,high=n-1;
                while(low<=high)
                {
                    mid=(low+high)/2;
                    if(arr[mid]==target) { return mid; }
                    else if(target>arr[mid]) { low=mid+1; }
                    else { high=mid-1; } 
                }
                return -1;
             }

Recursive Code: 

🔹 Why recursion fits naturally in Binary Search

Binary Search works by:

Looking at the middle of the array.

Deciding whether the answer lies in the left half or right half.

Ignoring the other half completely.

That means, after each step, the same problem (search in sorted array) reduces to a smaller subproblem (search in half array).

This is exactly the definition of recursion:
👉 “Solve a problem by solving a smaller version of the same problem.”

pseudo code: f(arr,low,high,target)
             {
                if(low>high) { return -1; }
                int mid = (low+high)/2;
                if(arr[mid]==target) { return mid; }
                else if(target>arr[mid]) { return f(arr,mid+1,high,target); }
                else { return f(arr,low,mid-1,target); }
             }

